  "id": "F1",
  "title": "Recovery isn't serialized with an active live cleanup",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "While one cleanup holds a descendant frozen, another command recovers its record and kills it. If the operating system reuses that process number and inspection becomes unavailable, the first cleanup can kill an unrelated process.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/cleanup-adjudication.yyRXVb/f1.ts\"",
    "output": "{\n  \"events\": [\n    \"A froze owned member\",\n    \"record count while A paused: 1\",\n    \"B recovery SIGKILLed member while A paused\",\n    \"B exit=0; original member stopped=true; records=0\",\n    \"A attempted SIGKILL after recovery finished, with birth inspection unavailable (signal suppressed)\"\n  ],\n  \"staleKill\": true,\n  \"result\": {\n    \"error\": \"Invocation termination could not be confirmed: injected inspection unavailable; injected inspection unavailable\"\n  }\n}"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, recovery executes `if (row.boot !== boot || stopped(row.pid) || (await processBirth(row.pid)) !== row.birth)` followed by `process.kill(row.pid, \"SIGKILL\")` without coordinating with live cleanup. Live cleanup sets `identity.frozen = freezing` at lines 494\u2013496, and its error path permits signalling when `birth === undefined && identity.frozen` at lines 535\u2013539. The probe used a real owned descendant, paused live inspection after SIGSTOP, and started another command in the same scope. Recovery killed the descendant and deleted its record; injected inspection failures then caused live cleanup to attempt the stale SIGKILL. That final signal was intercepted; actual PID reuse and killing an unrelated process were not attempted. Base contains the frozen fallback but has no recovery path providing this competing killer."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "While one command's cleanup has frozen its leftover processes and is still working on them, a second command starts in the same run (review finders and other parallel steps do this). The second command's recovery kills those processes and deletes their records. If the first cleanup's process inspection then fails, its fallback kills the same process IDs without checking them, and by then the system may have given one of those IDs to an unrelated process.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: the harmful outcome needs a precise interleaving of a concurrent recovery, the process being reaped, its process ID being reused, and the live cleanup's inspection then failing three times. That can't be triggered reliably in a throwaway script, so the evidence below is quoted code.",
   "evidence": "At head, stopMarkedProcesses writes each claimed member to process_cleanups before freezing it (`recorder?.recordProcessCleanup({... invocation: marker ...})`, then `identity.frozen = freezing`). It deletes the rows only when it finishes (`recorder?.deleteInvocationCleanups(marker)`). runProcess calls `recoverScopedProcesses()` before every command, and that function lists `store.listProcessCleanups(scope.runId, checkout)`. It has no check for whether an invocation's cleanup is still running, so it picks up the rows of a cleanup in progress, sends SIGKILL to members whose birth identity matches, waits until they are zombies or gone, and retires the rows. The live cleanup's catch path then runs `if (birth !== identity.birth && !(birth === undefined && identity.frozen)) continue; process.kill(pid, \"SIGKILL\")`. When inspection throws, `birth` is undefined, so a member still marked frozen is killed without an identity check. That relies on 'a frozen member cannot exit', and the concurrent recovery has broken that assumption. Concurrent invocations within one run exist (engine.ts:1520 Promise.all; review.ts:471 Promise.allSettled panel finders). Nothing in base or head serializes recovery against live cleanup."
  }
 },
